By building an Expense Tracker, you’ll practice:

Components
Props
State management
Forms
Lists
useEffect
API calls
Authentication
CRUD operations
React Router
Context API
Local storage
Backend integration
Charts and analytics

Stage 1 — Basic React Version

Start simple.

Features
Add expense:
Fetch the data
Store it in state
Display it with .map()
Delete expense
Show expense list
Show total amount

Concepts learned
useState
props
array .map()
forms
event handling

Stage 2 — Better UI + Categories

Add:

Categories
Date
Income vs Expense
Filter by category

Example categories:

Food
Rent
Transport
Entertainment
Concepts learned
controlled forms
filtering
conditional rendering

Stage 3 — Persist Data
Save data using: localStorage
Learn:

useEffect
persistence

Stage 4 — React Router

Add pages:

Dashboard
Add Expense
Reports
Profile
Login/Register

Learn:

navigation
protected routes

Stage 5 — Authentication

Add:

Register/Login
JWT authentication
Navbar changes after login

Stage 6 — Backend Integration
FastAPI (great choice since you're already using it)
Learn:

fetch/axios
async requests
tokens
authorization

Stage 7 — Analytics Dashboard

Add charts:

Monthly spending
Category breakdown
Income vs Expense

Use:

Recharts
Chart.js

This makes your project look professional instantly.

